.TH MONOAUTH 8
.SH NAME
monoauth \- authenticated, encrypted remote execution with Monocypher
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B listen \-a mono
.I addr command ...
.br
.B rcmd \-e mono
.I addr command ...
.br
.B cpu \-C mono
.I mach command ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mono
is an authentication and transport algorithm for
.IR listen (1),
.IR rcmd (1)
and
.IR cpu (1).
It is chosen by name instead of the keyring/SSL algorithms
.RB ( none ,
.BR rc4_128 ,
\&...).
The key material comes from
.IR certify (8):
a signer key in
.BR /keydb/mono-signer ,
and one identity file per user,
.BR /usr/\fIname\fP/keyring/mono ,
holding a name, a domain, a capability path, a 64-byte EdDSA secret
key and a 64-byte certificate.
.SS Handshake
The client sends a hello, the server answers with its hello and a
signature, and the client signs in return.  A hello is
.IP
.EX
version, name, pk, cert, ephemeral pk, domain, path, proof
.EE
.IP
with one-byte lengths for the strings and fixed 32- and 64-byte fields.
The certificate is an EdDSA signature over
.IR name || "\en" || pk ,
verified with the signer's public key (bytes 32 to 64 of
.BR mono-signer ).
The proof is
.IP
.EX
exp, epoch, time, window, root, epoch signature, leaf, index, path
.EE
.IP
where
.I path
is the Merkle inclusion path for the leaf
.IR "leaf(name, pk, exp)" .
The epoch signature covers
.IR "epoch1" || epoch || time || window || root
and is verified with the same signer, so a proof is only useful if it
was issued by the machine's signer.
.PP
Both hellos are hashed into a transcript.  The server signs the
transcript and the client verifies it with the server's key; the client
then signs the same transcript and the server verifies it with the
client's key.  Neither side has accepted the session until both
signatures check, so an intermediary cannot splice one hello into
another connection or replay an old exchange.
.PP
The ephemeral X25519 keys give a shared secret; BLAKE2b derives one key
per direction from the transcript, the shared secret and the role
labels
.B c2s
and
.BR s2c .
XChaCha20-Poly1305 protects each record, with a per-direction counter
as the nonce.
.SS Scope and revocation
.I domain
and
.I path
must be equal on both sides.  A server therefore only talks to clients
provisioned for the same capability, and a client only to a server in
its own scope.  Both default to
.B mono
and
.BR box/default ,
so existing identities interoperate until an administrator subdivides
them.
.PP
A peer that presents no proof is accepted only when its path begins
with
.BR box/ .
A local identity whose path is outside
.B box/
refuses to connect when it cannot build a proof: no epoch file, an
epoch signature that does not verify, an epoch older than its window,
a key that is not in the leaves, or an inclusion path that does not
reach the signed root.  The failure is therefore closed, not open: an
unreachable
.I monoca
reduces an identity to the
.B box/
namespace instead of granting it more.
.PP
Revocation is the absence of a leaf in a newer epoch: a revoked key
can no longer build a proof, and peers reject it.  Epochs are retained
under
.BR /keydb/monoca/roots/\fIn\fP ,
so a proof against an older root can be checked if one is presented
within its window.
.SS Transport
Records are a 4-byte length, a 16-byte tag and the ciphertext.  The
plaintext inside a record begins with a session tag and a frame type
(OPEN, DATA, FIN), so one link carries any number of sessions.  Each
session's plaintext side is one end of a loopback socket pair, giving
the caller one descriptor to read, write and pass to
.IR export (2).
A session is closed with a TCP
.B hangup
on that descriptor: dropping the last reference to a socket does not
signal the peer, but a hangup does, as a reset.
.PP
The authenticated name is passed to the command as
.B $user
in the
.IR listen (1)
context, exactly as with the keyring algorithms.
.SH EXAMPLE
Make a signer and an identity, then publish an epoch that authorises
it:
.IP
.EX
certify firstboot
certify user alice
monoca enroll alice
listen -a mono 'tcp!*!17010' /dis/sh.dis
.EE
.PP
.I monoca
reads the public key from the user's keyring when no key is given, and
.I certify
writes the default scope into the same file.  A deployment that wants
to separate services from users copies the identity file to another
name and edits the scope, for example:
.IP
.EX
cp /usr/alice/keyring/mono /usr/build/keyring/mono
echo 'domain build' >> /usr/build/keyring/mono
echo 'path svc'    >> /usr/build/keyring/mono
.EE
.PP
Both ends must then carry the same scope, or the handshake fails with
.B "mono: scope mismatch"
.SH FILES
.TF /usr/\fIname\fP/keyring/mono
.TP
.B /keydb/mono-signer
EdDSA signer key (private half; root only)
.TP
.BI /usr/ name /keyring/mono
name, domain, path, secret key and certificate
.TP
.B /keydb/monoca
epoch, leaves and retained roots from
.IR monoca (8)
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/monoauth.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR mono (2),
.IR merkle (2),
.IR certify (8),
.IR monoca (8),
.IR listen (1),
.IR rcmd (1),
.IR cpu (1)
.SH BUGS
Handshake version 2 is not compatible with earlier
.I monoauth
peers; both ends must be rebuilt.
.PP
Each connection carries one session for now:
.I listen
accepts a session from the link, runs the command, and closes it.  The
link layer itself multiplexes tagged sessions (OPEN, DATA, FIN) and has
been exercised with several sessions on one link, but the listener does
not yet accept more than one session per link, and
.I rcmd
opens a fresh link for each invocation.
.PP
There is no rekeying and no keepalive; the record counter space is 2 to
the 32.  An epoch is checked against the local clock only, so the clock
must be disciplined (see
.IR timed (8))
for proofs to remain valid.
